//? Title: Linde–Buzo–Gray vector quantization
//? Subtitle: Actual splits and Lloyd updates, original mixture and consistent final distortion
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A vector quantizer replaces a point by a nearby codeword. LBG grows its codebook
by splitting and then alternating nearest assignments with centroid updates. We
inspect the actual native stages, retain the original600-point mixture, expose
multiplicative zero-centroid collapse and correct the old mismatch between
returned centroids and pre-update assignments/distortion. Finite-budget results
are local fits, not global-optimal or distinct-center guarantees.
)

\toc

> Assignment and centroid steps

For points x_i and codewords c_k, mean squared Euclidean distortion is

$$D(C)=\frac1n\sum_i\min_k\|x_i-c_k\|^2.$$
\alttext(Each original point contributes its squared distance to the nearest actual codeword.)

For fixed codewords, choosing the nearest label minimizes the pointwise
objective. For a nonempty fixed assignment group, differentiating its sum of
squared distances gives c_k=mean(group), so the centroid update minimizes that
group’s assigned objective. Empty groups retain their old codewords. These two
facts explain nonincrease of the Lloyd objective within a fixed codebook size in
exact arithmetic; they do not prove a global optimum, across-size monotonicity
after perturbation, or a finite-precision stability guarantee.

Start at the global centroid, then split each c into
c*(1−epsilon),c*(1+epsilon). The historical loop doubles until size>=request, so
a non-power-of-two request rounds up, correcting the old “rounded down” comment.
Returned codebooks need not be nested, distinct or fully used. Multiplicative
perturbation leaves a zero centroid unchanged.

> Tiny actual split and centroid updates

For values1 and3 the initial mean is2. The real multiplicative split gives1.98
and2.02; nearest assignments then update the means to1 and3. The native trace
retains actual before codebooks, assignments/counts and committed after
codebooks. The diagram reads the first real two-codeword update rather than
reconstructing an optimization history.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lbg_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::linde_buzo_gray_trace({{1}, {3}}, 2);
    std::print("| actual stage size | actual completed Lloyd updates | actual final nearest distortion | " "actual tolerance stop |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (auto &s : t.stages)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.size, s.iterations,
                   s.distortion, s.tolerance_stop);
    auto i =
        std::find_if(t.iterations.begin(), t.iterations.end(), [](const auto &e) {
        return e.before.size() == 2;
    });
    std::print("\n| actual point | actual assigned before-codeword |\n| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::size_t j = 0; j < i->assignment.size(); ++j)
        std::print("| {} | {} |\n", t.data[j][0], i->assignment[j]);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("mean", {0, 0},
           std::format("Actual original mean\n{}", t.stages[0].final[0][0])).node("split", {6, 0}, std::format("Actual split codewords\n{}, {}", i->before[0][0], i->before[1][0])).node("updated", {12, 0}, std::format("Actual committed means\n{}, {}", i->after[0][0], i->after[1][0])).edge("mean", "split").edge("split", "updated").save_svg("build/lbg_tiny.svg");
}
```
