> Multiplicative zero-centroid collapse

The symmetric values−1 and1 have mean0. Every multiplicative child of0 is also0,
so this specific initialization never breaks the symmetry. Request3 rounds up
to4 identical zero codewords, earliest-index ties assign both points to
codeword0 and the remaining codewords are empty. Actual final distortion stays1.
This is a concrete failure of this splitting scheme, not evidence that four
well-chosen codewords could not quantize two values exactly. The plot shows both
original points and the four actual overlapping centers; the printed table
retains each duplicate identity.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lbg_zero.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto t = dm::algorithms::linde_buzo_gray_trace({{-1}, {1}}, 3);
    std::print("Original requested size {}; actual rounded size {}; actual final distortion {}.\n\n| actual " "codeword identity | actual codeword value | actual assigned point count |\n| ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n",
               t.analysis.requested_size, t.analysis.rounded_size,
               t.analysis.result.distortion);
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < t.analysis.result.codebook.size(); ++k) {
        auto c = t.analysis.result.codebook[k][0];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} |\n", k, c,
                   std::count(t.analysis.result.assignment.begin(),
                              t.analysis.result.assignment.end(), k));
        x.push_back(c);
        y.push_back(0);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points({-1, 1}, {0, 0}, "original points", {37, 99, 235}, 7).points(
        x, y, "overlapping centers", {220, 38, 38}, 9).x_limits(-1.5, 1.5).y_limits(-.5, 1).title("Actual multiplicative zero-centroid collapse").x_label("original or codeword coordinate").y_label("zero baseline").save_svg("build/lbg_zero.svg");
}
```
